Filmmaker Rian Johnson, best known for writing and directing "Knives Out," "Glass Onion," and "Star Wars: The Last Jedi," has seen his new iPhone photography app <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/chromagraph-camera/id6803329435">Chromagraph Camera</a> climb to the top of the paid <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/app-store/">App Store</a> charts after attracting attention on social media this week.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iPhone-18-Pro-Max-Glacier-Camera.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068468" /><br/>
Johnson developed the app with his longtime cinematographer Steve Yedlin and programmer Eric Cameron, bringing image processing techniques from their films to iPhone still photography. Chromagraph currently ranks No. 1 in Photo & Video on the <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/chromagraph-camera/id6803329435">U.S. App Store</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Johnson's announcement on X accumulated six million views within a day, while users have been sharing their results, with some photo posts attracting thousands of likes.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><blockquote class="twitter-tweet"><a href="https://twitter.com/rianjohnson/status/2115436064458621189"></a></blockquote><script async src="https://platform.twitter.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"></script></div><br/>
<br/>
Chromagraph shoots RAW photos and processes them using techniques Yedlin developed for his films, giving pictures a distinctive treatment of color, contrast, and grain. It also adds halation, a soft glow around bright areas. Users can adjust the look with controls for color, tone, and texture.<br/>
<br/>
The effect appears after taking a photo, when users open the app's gallery. Original RAW files are kept intact, so edits can be changed later.<br/>
<br/>
For sharing, photographs can be exported as JPEG or HEIC files with the processing applied. An automatic export option also sends each finished photo to the user's photo library.<br/>
<br/>
